\ Bounded-memory regression for spawn-detached.
\
\ Spawn many detached tasks inside one still-open scope under a low memory
\ cap. Each detached task is reaped at its own completion, so its task
\ struct, context, and arena are freed and steady-state memory stays
\ bounded. The two `yield`s let the just-spawned task run to completion and
\ be reaped before the next iteration, so at most a couple of tasks are ever
\ live and peak memory is a few MB, well under the 8 MB cap set in the
\ `.flags` sidecar.
\
\ Before reaping existed, every completed child was retained until scope
\ exit, so a regression back to that behavior exhausts the cap partway through the loop and the
\ run dies with a memory-limit error instead of printing the marker. The harness runs
\ integration tests single-worker by default, which makes the reap-before-next-spawn pacing
\ deterministic.
\
\ The iteration count is sized empirically: with the completion-time reap disabled, the cap
\ trips between 2000 and 3000 iterations, so 8000 fails reliably while running about 1.5 seconds
\ standalone against the 10-second harness cap. That margin survives a fully loaded parallel
\ suite run.

[
  8000 [
    [ 1 1 + drop ] spawn-detached
    yield yield
  ] times
] task-scope

"bounded" print-line
